	    </Examples>
    </Classification--> <!-- ENDOF:  id="c5"           ~~
                          ~~ one beHind reuse          ~~
                          ~~ ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ -->
    
    <Classification id="c6">
      <DisplayName>cover Wraparound reuse</DisplayName>
	    <KeyboardShortcut>W</KeyboardShortcut>
	    <DirectoryName>Cover_Wraparound_Reuse</DirectoryName>
	    <AddedFilenameEnding>cwa</AddedFilenameEnding>
	    <Description>
        Reused exterior-cover material continues around the edge of a cover
        board onto its inner side. Use CWA when at least one point of the
        inside edge of this wraparound is visible. Otherwise use TBR; when
        uncertain between these two, prefer TBR.

        The legacy description includes wraparound visible on the main
        surface, on the immediately opposite partial surface across the
        gutter, and in views of the head, tail, or fore-edge. Apply the
        current visible-edge criterion rather than automatically labeling
        every glimpse of cover material CWA. Retain other applicable
        view/context labels under the current multiple-class rules. Obsolete
        combined keys and the legacy one-behind category are not current
        instructions.
      </Description>
	    <Examples>
        In other file
	    </Examples>
      
    </Classification> <!-- ENDOF:  id="c6"             ~~
                        ~~ cover Wraparound reuse      ~~
                        ~~ ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ -->
    
    <Classification id="c7">
      <DisplayName>Tiny background reuse</DisplayName>
	    <KeyboardShortcut>T</KeyboardShortcut>
	    <DirectoryName>Tiny_Background_Reuse</DirectoryName>
	    <AddedFilenameEnding>tbr</AddedFilenameEnding>
	    <Description>
        Reuse is visible in the background beyond intervening pages, often at
        the edge of a paste-down, flyleaf, or cover wraparound while the main
        view shows pages within the book. It can be a small exposed margin
        behind the stack of leaves. For the CWA/TBR distinction, use CWA when
        the inside edge of the wraparound is visible; otherwise use TBR. When
        uncertain between TBR and CWA or connecting/guard reuse, prefer TBR.

        The old HTML's one-leaf versus two-leaf cutoff depended on the omitted
        one-behind class. Do not reactivate that category or infer new labels
        from that old counting rule. Apply the current context and combination
        instructions to any other visibly qualifying reuse.
      </Description>
	    <Examples>
        In other file
	    </Examples>
      
    </Classification> <!-- ENDOF:  id="c7"             ~~
                        ~~ Tiny background reuse       ~~
                        ~~ ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ -->
    
    <Classification id="c8">
      <DisplayName>Connecting or guard reuse</DisplayName>
	    <KeyboardShortcut>C</KeyboardShortcut>
	    <DirectoryName>Connecting_or_Guard_Reuse</DirectoryName>
	    <AddedFilenameEnding>scg</AddedFilenameEnding>
	    <Description>
        Reused information-bearing material serves a connecting,
        strengthening, or guarding role: for example, strips connecting
        gatherings to the binding, material associated with binding straps,
        stubs between gatherings that reduce friction, or guards providing a
        sewing support. It need not be small. Keep the existing scg token and
        current directory and display name.

        At a gutter tear, use UCR unless spine protection is apparent; do not
        infer a connecting/guard function merely because material is exposed.
        When uncertain between SCG and TBR, the current guide favors TBR. A
        triangular cut or folded cover flap alone does not establish SCG; the
        two triangle-cut cover examples reviewed on November 17 are excluded
        from SCG. Their purpose is not established.
      </Description>
	    <Examples>
        In other file
	    </Examples>
      
    </Classification> <!-- ENDOF:  id="c8"             ~~
                        ~~ Connecting or guard reuse   ~~
                        ~~ ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ -->
    
    <Classification id="c9">
      <DisplayName>across book Gutter reuse</DisplayName>
	    <KeyboardShortcut>G</KeyboardShortcut>
	    <DirectoryName>Across_Book_Gutter_Reuse</DirectoryName>
	    <AddedFilenameEnding>abg</AddedFilenameEnding>
	    <Description>
        A view marker for reuse or another interesting phenomenon visible on
        the facing page across the book gutter when the image mainly presents
        one page. Assign the relevant phenomenon/context classification as
        well. ABG alone is not a positive binding-reuse classification.

        ABG does not count as another binding-reuse subtype for MBR. G+F does
        not warrant B, whereas G+F+R does under the existing guide convention.
        Retain the current MCL/MMX rules when other interesting phenomena are
        involved; do not revive the legacy combined-category shortcut.
      </Description>
	    <Examples>
        In other file
	    </Examples>
      
    </Classification> <!-- ENDOF:  id="c9"             ~~
                        ~~ across book Gutter reuse    ~~
                        ~~ ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ -->
    
    
    <Classification id="c10">
      <DisplayName>wrapper reuse</DisplayName>
	    <KeyboardShortcut>4</KeyboardShortcut>
	    <DirectoryName>Wrapper_Reuse</DirectoryName>
	    <AddedFilenameEnding>wpr</AddedFilenameEnding>
	    <Description>
        Information-bearing reused material wraps around or holds loose papers
        without sewing, in the manner of a folded folder or wrapper. The
        illustrated guide supplies the examples and distinctions from a bound
        limp-vellum cover.

        Wrapper examples and the wrapper prediction output are excluded from
        the current MVP experiment; the ontology entry remains. The guide's
        wrapper/FMR same-piece MBR captions conflict and remain unresolved. Do
        not resolve them by automatically counting tags or by extending the
        November 17 UCR/FMR slit clarification to wrapper examples.
      </Description>
	    <Examples>
        In other file
	    </Examples>
      
    </Classification> <!-- ENDOF:  id="c10"            ~~
                        ~~ wrapper reuse               ~~
                        ~~ ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ -->
    
    <Classification id="c15">
      <DisplayName>general not in situ reuse</DisplayName>
	    <KeyboardShortcut>J</KeyboardShortcut>
	    <DirectoryName>General_Not_In_Situ_Reuse</DirectoryName>
	    <AddedFilenameEnding>gni</AddedFilenameEnding>
	    <Description>
        Binding reuse no longer in its in-situ binding context. The current
        general category combines the former detached cover, front/back
        matter, spine-protection, and connecting/guard categories c11-c14. Use
        J and gni; the former separate keys and the older Y instruction are
        historical. This combined category avoids requiring a reliable
        identification of the detached fragment's more specific former role.
      </Description>
	    <Examples>
        In other file
	    </Examples>
      
    </Classification> <!-- ENDOF:  id="c15"                   ~~
                        ~~ general not in situ reuse   ~~
                        ~~ ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ -->
    
    
    <!-- ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ -->
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
